<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-QR-factorization">
  <title><m>QR</m> factorization and least squares</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-qr-definition">
    <title>From Gram–Schmidt to <m>QR</m></title>

    <p>
      The <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt">Gram–Schmidt algorithm</xref> processes the independent columns of a matrix from left to right. It constructs orthonormal vectors <m>\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_n</m> and records coefficients <m>r_{ij}</m>. These same quantities form a <term>matrix factorization</term>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let
      <me>
        A
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathbf{a}_1\amp\mathbf{a}_2\amp\cdots\amp\mathbf{a}_n
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      At column <m>j</m>, the Gram–Schmidt algorithm computes
      <me>
        \mathbf{p}_j
        =
        \sum_{i\lt j}
        r_{ij}\mathbf{q}_i,
        \qquad
        \mathbf{v}_j
        =
        \mathbf{a}_j-\mathbf{p}_j,
      </me>
      <me>
        r_{jj}
        =
        \|\mathbf{v}_j\|,
        \qquad
        \mathbf{q}_j
        =
        \frac{\mathbf{v}_j}{r_{jj}}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Since
      <me>
        \mathbf{v}_j
        =
        r_{jj}\mathbf{q}_j,
      </me>
      we can reconstruct the original column:
      <me>
        \mathbf{a}_j
        =
        \mathbf{p}_j+\mathbf{v}_j
        =
        \sum_{i\lt j}
        r_{ij}\mathbf{q}_i
        +
        r_{jj}\mathbf{q}_j
        =
        \sum_{i\leq j}
        r_{ij}\mathbf{q}_i.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Thus column <m>j</m> uses only <m>\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_j</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Collect the orthonormal vectors in
      <me>
        Q
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathbf{q}_1\amp\mathbf{q}_2\amp\cdots\amp\mathbf{q}_n
        \end{bmatrix}
      </me>
      and the coefficients in
      <me>
        R
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        r_{11}\amp r_{12}\amp\cdots\amp r_{1n}\\
        0\amp r_{22}\amp\cdots\amp r_{2n}\\
        \vdots\amp\ddots\amp\ddots\amp\vdots\\
        0\amp\cdots\amp0\amp r_{nn}
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The column equations above become the single matrix equation
      <me>
        A=QR.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The zeros below the diagonal of <m>R</m> record the order of the algorithm: column <m>j</m> uses only directions constructed by stage <m>j</m>.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-3">
      <title>Reduced <m>QR</m> factorization</title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-3-1">
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m\times n</m> matrix with <term>linearly independent columns</term>. A <term>reduced <m>QR</m> factorization</term> of <m>A</m> is a factorization
          <me>
            A=QR,
          </me>
          where
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><m>Q</m> is an <m>m\times n</m> matrix with <term>orthonormal columns</term>;</p></li>
          <li><p><m>R</m> is an invertible <term>upper-triangular</term> <m>n\times n</m> matrix.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For the Gram–Schmidt convention used in this unit, the diagonal entries of <m>R</m> are positive:
          <me>
            r_{jj}=\|\mathbf{v}_j\|>0.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <activity xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-7">
      <title>Reading <m>Q^T Q</m> (U4-LO1)</title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-7-1">
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            Q
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1/\sqrt{3}\amp1/\sqrt{6}\\
            1/\sqrt{3}\amp-2/\sqrt{6}\\
            1/\sqrt{3}\amp1/\sqrt{6}
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{q}_1\amp\mathbf{q}_2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute <m>\|\mathbf{q}_1\|^2</m> and <m>\|\mathbf{q}_2\|^2</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute <m>\mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_2</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Compute <m>Q^TQ</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What does the <m>(i,j)</m>-entry of <m>Q^TQ</m> measure?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Explain why
              <me>
                Q^TQ=I_2
              </me>
              is the matrix check that the columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-7-2">
        <p>
          We have
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{q}_1\|^2
            =
            \frac13+\frac13+\frac13
            =
            1
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{q}_2\|^2
            =
            \frac16+\frac46+\frac16
            =
            1.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Also,
          <me>
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_2
            =
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{18}}
            -
            \frac{2}{\sqrt{18}}
            +
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{18}}
            =
            0.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            Q^TQ
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_1\amp
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_2\\
            \mathbf{q}_2^T\mathbf{q}_1\amp
            \mathbf{q}_2^T\mathbf{q}_2
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\amp0\\
            0\amp1
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            I_2.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The <m>(i,j)</m>-entry of <m>Q^TQ</m> is the dot product <m>\mathbf{q}_i^T\mathbf{q}_j</m>. The diagonal entries check unit length, and the off-diagonal entries check orthogonality.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <theorem xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-10">
      <title>Gram–Schmidt produces <m>QR</m></title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-10-1">
        <p>
          Every <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> with <term>linearly independent columns</term> has a reduced <m>QR</m> factorization
          <me>
            A=QR
          </me>
          in which the columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal and <m>R</m> is upper triangular with positive diagonal entries.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-10-2">
        <p>
          Apply the <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt">Gram–Schmidt algorithm</xref> to the columns
          <me>
            \mathbf{a}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{a}_n.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          At stage <m>j</m>, the algorithm gives
          <me>
            \mathbf{a}_j
            =
            \sum_{i\leq j}
            r_{ij}\mathbf{q}_i.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Collecting these equations column by column gives
          <me>
            A=QR.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal. The condition <m>i\leq j</m> makes <m>R</m> upper triangular, and
          <me>
            r_{jj}
            =
            \|\mathbf{v}_j\|
            >
            0
          </me>
          because the input columns are linearly independent.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <corollary xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-13">
      <title>Square case</title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-13-1">
        <p>
          Every <term>invertible square matrix</term> has a <m>QR</m> factorization
          <me>
            A=QR,
          </me>
          where <m>Q</m> is an orthogonal matrix and <m>R</m> is upper triangular with positive diagonal entries.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          An invertible square matrix has linearly independent columns. Apply <q><xref ref="subsec-qr-definition-10">Gram–Schmidt produces QR</xref>.</q> Since <m>Q</m> is square and has orthonormal columns,
          <me>
            Q^TQ=QQ^T=I.
          </me>
          Thus <m>Q</m> is an orthogonal matrix.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </corollary>

    <activity xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-11">
      <title>Build <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m> from the Gram–Schmidt ledger (U4-LO2, U4-LO6)</title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-11-1">
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            A
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\amp1\\
            4\amp2
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{a}_1\amp\mathbf{a}_2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute
              <me>
                r_{11}=\|\mathbf{a}_1\|
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                \mathbf{q}_1
                =
                \frac{\mathbf{a}_1}{r_{11}}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the projection coefficient
              <me>
                r_{12}
                =
                \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{a}_2.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute
              <me>
                \mathbf{p}_2
                =
                r_{12}\mathbf{q}_1
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                \mathbf{v}_2
                =
                \mathbf{a}_2-\mathbf{p}_2.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute
              <me>
                r_{22}
                =
                \|\mathbf{v}_2\|
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                \mathbf{q}_2
                =
                \frac{\mathbf{v}_2}{r_{22}}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Assemble
              <me>
                Q
                =
                \begin{bmatrix}
                \mathbf{q}_1\amp\mathbf{q}_2
                \end{bmatrix}
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                R
                =
                \begin{bmatrix}
                r_{11}\amp r_{12}\\
                0\amp r_{22}
                \end{bmatrix}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Check
              <me>
                Q^TQ=I_2
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                QR=A.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-11-2">
        <p>
          The first column gives
          <me>
            r_{11}
            =
            \|\mathbf{a}_1\|
            =
            5,
            \qquad
            \mathbf{q}_1
            =
            \frac15
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\\
            4
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The projection coefficient is
          <me>
            r_{12}
            =
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{a}_2
            =
            \frac35(1)+\frac45(2)
            =
            \frac{11}{5}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            \mathbf{p}_2
            =
            r_{12}\mathbf{q}_1
            =
            \frac{11}{5}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/5\\
            4/5
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            33/25\\
            44/25
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}_2
            =
            \mathbf{a}_2-\mathbf{p}_2
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            2
            \end{bmatrix}
            -
            \begin{bmatrix}
            33/25\\
            44/25
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            -8/25\\
            6/25
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Its length is
          <me>
            r_{22}
            =
            \|\mathbf{v}_2\|
            =
            \frac{2}{5},
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            \mathbf{q}_2
            =
            \frac{\mathbf{v}_2}{r_{22}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            -4/5\\
            3/5
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Thus
          <me>
            Q
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/5\amp-4/5\\
            4/5\amp3/5
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            R
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            5\amp11/5\\
            0\amp2/5
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The orthonormality check gives
          <me>
            Q^TQ
            =
            I_2,
          </me>
          and direct multiplication gives
          <me>
            QR
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\amp1\\
            4\amp2
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            A.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-12">
      <title>An upper-triangular matrix already has a <m>QR</m> factorization (U4-LO6)</title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-12-1">
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            A
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            6\amp2\\
            0\amp3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Explain why
          <me>
            Q=I_2,
            \qquad
            R=A
          </me>
          is a <m>QR</m> factorization. Check each condition in the definition.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-12-2">
        <p>
          The columns of <m>I_2</m> are orthonormal, so
          <me>
            Q^TQ=I_2.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The matrix
          <me>
            R
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            6\amp2\\
            0\amp3
            \end{bmatrix}
          </me>
          is upper triangular and has positive diagonal entries. Finally,
          <me>
            QR
            =
            I_2A
            =
            A.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Thus <m>A=QR</m> is a <m>QR</m> factorization.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <warning xml:id="warn-u4-rectangular-q">
      <title>Rectangular <m>Q</m></title>
      <p>
        In a reduced <m>QR</m> factorization of an <m>m\times n</m> matrix with <m>m\gt n</m>, the matrix <m>Q</m> has orthonormal columns, so
        <me>
          Q^TQ=I_n.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        However, <m>Q</m> is not square, and generally
        <me>
          QQ^T\neq I_m.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead,
        <me>
          QQ^T\mathbf{b}
          =
          \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{col}(Q)}(\mathbf{b})
          =
          \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{col}(A)}(\mathbf{b}).
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Indeed, the entries of <m>Q^T\mathbf{b}</m> are the dot products <m>\mathbf{q}_i\cdot\mathbf{b}</m>. Multiplying by <m>Q</m> forms the corresponding linear combination of its columns:
        <me>
          QQ^T\mathbf{b}
          =
          \sum_{i=1}^n(\mathbf{q}_i\cdot\mathbf{b})\mathbf{q}_i
          =
          \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{col}(Q)}(\mathbf{b}).
        </me>
        The last equality follows from the <xref ref="thm-6-2projection">projection theorem</xref>, since the columns of <m>Q</m> form an orthonormal basis of <m>\operatorname{col}(Q)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Also, <m>A=QR</m> expresses every column of <m>A</m> as a linear combination of the columns of <m>Q</m>. Since <m>R</m> is invertible, <m>Q=AR^{-1}</m> gives the reverse inclusion. Thus <m>\operatorname{col}(Q)=\operatorname{col}(A)</m>, so the two projections agree.
      </p>
    </warning>

    <warning>
      <title>Signs in a <m>QR</m> factorization</title>
      <p>
        The <term>positive-diagonal convention</term> fixes the signs of the Gram–Schmidt factorization. Without that convention, one may replace a column <m>\mathbf{q}_j</m> by <m>-\mathbf{q}_j</m> and replace row <m>j</m> of <m>R</m> by its negative. The product <m>QR</m> does not change. Numerical software may therefore return <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m> with signs different from a hand calculation. Check <m>Q^TQ=I</m> and <m>QR=A</m> rather than requiring every entry to have the same sign.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="bridge-qr-least-squares">
    <title>Least squares with <m>QR</m></title>

    <p>
      The normal equations in <xref ref="thm-normal-equations-residual-orthogonality" text="type-global"/> give a direct route from a <m>QR</m> factorization to a least-squares solution.
    </p>
    <p>
      Suppose
      <me>
        A=QR
      </me>
      is a reduced <m>QR</m> factorization. The normal equations are
      <me>
        A^TA\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
        =
        A^T\mathbf{b}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Substituting <m>A=QR</m> gives
      <me>
        R^TQ^TQR\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
        =
        R^TQ^T\mathbf{b}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Since
      <me>
        Q^TQ=I,
      </me>
      this becomes
      <me>
        R^TR\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
        =
        R^TQ^T\mathbf{b}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The matrix <m>R</m> is invertible, so <m>R^T</m> is invertible. Canceling <m>R^T</m> gives
      <me>
        R\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
        =
        Q^T\mathbf{b}.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      The next activity shows why this upper-triangular system is easy to solve by back-substitution, the method introduced in <xref ref="ex-u2-back-substitution" text="type-global"/> in Unit 2.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-8">
      <title>Back-substitution (U2-LO2)</title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-8-1">
        <p>
          Solve
          <me>
            R\mathbf{x}
            =
            \mathbf{y},
          </me>
          where
          <me>
            R
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4\amp2\amp7\\
            0\amp3\amp5\\
            0\amp0\amp2
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{y}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            29\\
            21\\
            6
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Work from the last equation upward.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-8-2">
        <p>
          The last equation gives
          <me>
            2x_3=6,
            \qquad
            x_3=3.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The second equation gives
          <me>
            3x_2+5x_3=21,
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            3x_2+15=21,
            \qquad
            x_2=2.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The first equation gives
          <me>
            4x_1+2x_2+7x_3=29,
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            4x_1+4+21=29,
            \qquad
            x_1=1.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            \mathbf{x}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            2\\
            3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <algorithm xml:id="alg-u4-qr-least-squares">
      <title>Least squares with <m>QR</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <strong>Input.</strong> An <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> with linearly independent columns and a vector <m>\mathbf{b}\in\mathbb{R}^m</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute a reduced <m>QR</m> factorization
              <me>
                A=QR.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute
              <me>
                \mathbf{y}=Q^T\mathbf{b}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Solve the upper-triangular system
              <me>
                R\widehat{\mathbf{x}}=\mathbf{y}
              </me>
              by back-substitution.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          <strong>Output.</strong> The vector <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m> is the least-squares coefficient vector. The fitted vector is
          <me>
            A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            QR\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            QQ^T\mathbf{b}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>

    <p>
      This method avoids forming <m>A^TA</m> or computing a matrix inverse: once <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m> are known, we need only dot products and back-substitution. If <m>A</m> stays fixed while <m>\mathbf{b}</m> changes, we can reuse the same factorization for each new right-hand side. In floating-point computation, a numerically stable <m>QR</m> routine can also be more accurate than solving the normal equations, especially when the columns of <m>A</m> are nearly linearly dependent.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="act-u4-qr-least-squares-by-hand">
      <title>Solving least squares with <m>QR</m> (U4-LO2, U4-LO6)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use <m>QR</m> to fit a line <m>y=c_0+c_1t</m> to the data points
          <me>
            (0,1),\qquad(1,2),\qquad(2,2)
          </me>
          from <xref ref="act-u4-fit-line-three-points" text="type-global"/>. The design matrix and data vector are
          <me>
            X=\begin{bmatrix}
            1\amp0\\
            1\amp1\\
            1\amp2
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{y}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\2\\2\end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>Apply Gram–Schmidt to the columns of <m>X</m> to find a reduced factorization <m>X=QR</m> with positive diagonal entries in <m>R</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Compute <m>Q^T\mathbf{y}</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Solve <m>R\widehat{\mathbf{c}}=Q^T\mathbf{y}</m> by back-substitution and write the fitted line.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Write <m>X=[\mathbf{a}_1\ \mathbf{a}_2]</m>. The first column gives
          <me>
            r_{11}=\|\mathbf{a}_1\|=\sqrt{3},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{q}_1=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\1\end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For the second column,
          <me>
            r_{12}=\mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{a}_2
            =\frac{0+1+2}{\sqrt{3}}=\sqrt{3}.
          </me>
          Subtracting its component along <m>\mathbf{q}_1</m> gives
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}_2
            =\mathbf{a}_2-r_{12}\mathbf{q}_1
            =\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\\2\end{bmatrix}
            -\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\1\end{bmatrix}
            =\begin{bmatrix}-1\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Hence
          <me>
            r_{22}=\|\mathbf{v}_2\|=\sqrt{2},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{q}_2=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}-1\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Thus the reduced factors are
          <me>
            Q=\begin{bmatrix}
            1/\sqrt{3}\amp-1/\sqrt{2}\\
            1/\sqrt{3}\amp0\\
            1/\sqrt{3}\amp1/\sqrt{2}
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            R=\begin{bmatrix}
            \sqrt{3}\amp\sqrt{3}\\
            0\amp\sqrt{2}
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The transformed right-hand side is
          <me>
            Q^T\mathbf{y}
            =\begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{y}\\
            \mathbf{q}_2^T\mathbf{y}
            \end{bmatrix}
            =\begin{bmatrix}
            (1+2+2)/\sqrt{3}\\
            (-1+0+2)/\sqrt{2}
            \end{bmatrix}
            =\begin{bmatrix}
            5/\sqrt{3}\\
            1/\sqrt{2}
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The last equation of <m>R\widehat{\mathbf{c}}=Q^T\mathbf{y}</m> gives
          <me>
            \sqrt{2}\,\widehat c_1=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}},
            \qquad
            \widehat c_1=\frac12.
          </me>
          Substituting into the first equation gives
          <me>
            \sqrt{3}\,\widehat c_0+\sqrt{3}\left(\frac12\right)
            =\frac{5}{\sqrt{3}},
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            \widehat c_0=\frac53-\frac12=\frac76.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{c}}=\begin{bmatrix}7/6\\1/2\end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and the fitted line is
          <me>
            y=\frac76+\frac12t.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u4-qr-code-reading">
      <title>Solving least squares with <m>QR</m> by code (U4-LO6, U4-LO7)</title>
      <statement xml:id="act-u4-qr-code-reading-2">
        <p>
          The following code repeats the <m>QR</m> least-squares calculation from <xref ref="act-u4-qr-least-squares-by-hand" text="type-global"/>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The function <c>np.linalg.qr</c> returns the two factors, which the assignment stores in <c>Q</c> and <c>R</c>: <c>Q</c> has orthonormal columns, <c>R</c> is upper triangular, and their product is <c>X</c>. The option <c>mode="reduced"</c> keeps only the columns of <c>Q</c> needed to span the column space of <c>X</c>, together with the corresponding square factor <c>R</c>, rather than extending <c>Q</c> to a square orthogonal matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          The expression <c>np.eye(Q.shape[1])</c> constructs an identity matrix whose size is the number of columns of <c>Q</c>; here it is the <m>2\times2</m> identity matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          The function <c>np.linalg.solve</c> solves a square system directly, without explicitly computing the inverse of its coefficient matrix. Using it is preferable to forming an inverse and then multiplying, which adds unnecessary computation and can introduce additional rounding error.
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

t = np.array([0.0, 1.0, 2.0])
y = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 2.0])

X = np.column_stack([np.ones_like(t), t])

Q, R = np.linalg.qr(X, mode="reduced")

c_qr = np.linalg.solve(R, Q.T @ y)
c_lstsq = np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond=None)[0]

(
    Q.shape,
    R.shape,
    np.allclose(Q.T @ Q, np.eye(Q.shape[1])),
    np.allclose(Q @ R, X),
    c_qr,
    c_lstsq,
    np.allclose(c_qr, c_lstsq),
)
]]></code></program>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>What should <c>Q.shape</c> and <c>R.shape</c> be?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What does</p>
            <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q.T @ Q
]]></code></program>
            <p>check?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What does</p>
            <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q @ R
]]></code></program>
            <p>check?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What mathematical vector is computed by</p>
            <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q.T @ y
]]></code></program>
            <p>?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Why does the code solve a system with <c>R</c>?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Why should <c>c_qr</c> and <c>c_lstsq</c> agree?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Does their agreement mean that
              <me>
                X\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{y}
              </me>
              has an exact solution?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Why may the entries of <c>Q</c> and <c>R</c> have signs different from a hand Gram–Schmidt calculation?</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="act-u4-qr-code-reading-3">
        <p>
          Since <c>X</c> has shape <c>(3, 2)</c>, reduced <m>QR</m> gives
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q.shape == (3, 2)
R.shape == (2, 2)
]]></code></program>
        <p>The expression</p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q.T @ Q
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          checks whether the columns of <c>Q</c> are orthonormal. The expression
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q @ R
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          checks whether the factors reconstruct <c>X</c>.
        </p>
        <p>The vector</p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q.T @ y
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          is the right-hand side <m>Q^T\mathbf{y}</m> in the reduced system
          <me>
            R\widehat{\mathbf{c}}
            =
            Q^T\mathbf{y}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The matrix <c>R</c> is square, invertible, and upper triangular, so the code solves this system with <c>np.linalg.solve</c>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both methods compute the same least-squares coefficient vector, so
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[c_qr
]]></code></program>
        <p>and</p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[c_lstsq
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          should both be close to
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[array([1.16666667, 0.5])
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          and the final <c>np.allclose</c> check should return <c>True</c>.
        </p>
        <p>
          This agreement does not mean that
          <me>
            X\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{y}
          </me>
          has an exact solution. The residual from the <xref ref="act-u4-fit-line-three-points">regression activity</xref> is nonzero.
        </p>
        <p>
          A valid <m>QR</m> factorization can use simultaneous sign changes in one column of <m>Q</m> and the corresponding row of <m>R</m>. Therefore numerical output may have different signs from a hand calculation while still satisfying
          <me>
            Q^TQ=I
            \qquad\text{and}\qquad
            QR=X.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

  </subsection>
</section>
